    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All changes complete and the build passes (2/2 tests green).\n\n## Summary\n\nI added a structured address form to owner creation, keeping the flat `address` accepted for backward compatibility.\n\n**Contract (`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`)**\n- Added optional `addressLine1` and `addressLine2` to the `OwnerFields` schema (so both request and response DTOs carry them).\n- Removed `address` from the `required` list, so a structured-only owner passes bean validation. `firstName`, `lastName`, `city`, `telephone` stay required \u2014 earlier minimal owners still work.\n\n**Persistence (`Owner.java` + all four `db/*/schema.sql`)**\n- Added `addressLine1` / `addressLine2` fields and `address_line1` / `address_line2` columns so the structured lines round-trip to the response.\n\n**Address logic (`AddressNormalizer.java`)** \u2014 centralized in the existing helper:\n- `isPresent` \u2014 non-blank check.\n- `compose(line1, line2)` \u2014 the composed string: normalized `addressLine1`, plus a single space and normalized `addressLine2` when present.\n- `canonical(line1, line2, flat)` \u2014 prefers the structured composition when `addressLine1` is present, otherwise the normalized flat address. This is the single \"prefer structured, fall back to flat\" rule everything downstream reads.\n\n**Pipeline steps**\n- `NormalizeOwnerAddress` \u2014 normalizes whichever structured fields are supplied in place, then sets `address` to the canonical value.\n- `ValidateNewOwner` \u2014 accepts an owner whose address is present in *either* form (non-blank `addressLine1` or a flat `address`), plus city.\n- `ApplyOwner` (PUT path) \u2014 copies the structured lines and composes `address` the same way.\n\nBecause `NormalizeOwnerAddress` writes the canonical address back onto the shared request before `BuildOwner`, every later reader (mapping, identity/household derivation, response) sees the one preferred form. The `GET` response returns the composed `address` (e.g. `addressLine1: \"10 king st\"` \u2192 `address: \"10 KING STREET\"`), matching the new acceptance tests.\n\nI confirmed via grep that no code in this variant reads the address field for the household hash, postcode validation, or locality (they key off last name/postcode/city), so there was nothing else to redirect to the structured fields.",
